The $1.7 billion and $5.9 billion increases in total operating costs and expenses during the three and nine months ended September 30, 2026, respectively, as compared to the same periods of 2025 were primarily attributable to:

• $903 million and $2.0 billion increases, respectively, in cost of natural gas feedstock, largely due to increased volume of LNG delivered and to a lesser degree, increased U.S. natural gas prices and global LNG pricing, as referenced in the Revenues discussion above, between the nine month periods;

• $618 million and $3.4 billion, respectively, of unfavorable changes in the fair value of agreements accounted for as derivative instruments recognized in cost of sales, of which $471 million and $3.2 billion, respectively, related to the long-term IPM agreements designated as NPNS in June 2026, as further described above under the caption Net income (loss). The unfavorable variance between the three month periods was primarily attributable to favorable fair value changes recognized on these agreements in 2025 that did not recur following the NPNS designation because these agreements were no longer subject to recurring fair value measurement.
